    The paper "A practical analytic method for calculating π(x)" by Jens Franke, Thorsten Kleinjung, Jan Büthe, and Alexander Jost was published in Mathematics of Computation, volume 86, number 308, pages 2889–2909 (2017).
    
    This paper describes a practical analytic method for the computation of π(x), the number of prime numbers ≤x, which is similar to the method proposed by Lagarias and Odlyzko but uses the Weil-Barner explicit formula.
    
    The search results also reference this work in the context of analytic computation of π(x). The paper addresses the fact that the explicit formula (1.2) converges too slowly to be of direct practical use for calculating π(x), and to obtain a practical method, they subtract a sum over zeros of a function with similar asymptotic behavior, which can be transformed by the Weil-Barner formula into a sum over primes.
    
    The paper represents an important contribution to efficient computational methods for calculating the prime counting function using analytic techniques involving the Riemann zeta function zeros and truncation error analysis.
    
    
