    I found several resources related to the Lagarias-Odlyzko analytic method for computing π(x). Here's what I found:
    
    ## Overview
    
    The Lagarias-Odlyzko analytic algorithm for computing π(x)—the number of primes up to x—is asymptotically the fastest known algorithm for the computation of π(x). It uses numerical integration of a function related to the Riemann zeta function, in combination with a summation involving a "kernel" function evaluated at prime powers near x.
    
    ## Algorithm Complexity
    
    For each b with 0 ≤b ≤1/4 and each ε > 0 there is an algorithm A(b,ε) which computes π(x) using O(x^(3/5 −2b/5 + ε)) bit operations and uses O(x^(b + ε)) bits of storage. In particular one can compute π(x) using O(x^(3/5 + ε)) bit operations and O(x^ε) space, or using O(x^(1/2 + ε)) bit operations and O(x^(1/4 + ε)) space.
    
    ## Truncation Error Analysis
    
    The work includes a careful analysis of various sources of truncation error and provides choices of parameters which bound the truncation error while keeping computation to a minimum.
    
    The search results point to the original Lagarias-Odlyzko paper ("Computing π(x): An Analytic Method") and related implementations. For the specific explicit error bound with the form "c√(x) log² x" you mentioned, you would likely find this in the detailed analysis sections of the original paper or in subsequent implementations like those by Platt or in dissertations on the topic.
    
    
